    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d786">

                <pb facs="0887" n="775" xml:id="pg775"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.6359 Antimony/18</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 3441</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-03-19">March 19, 1935</date>.<lb/>[Received April
                        20.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to this
                    Legation’s telegram No. 86 of February 23, 12 noon,<anchor n="62"
                        xml:id="d786-f1"/> concerning the Hunan Antimony Syndicate, and to enclose
                    herewith a copy of despatch L. No. 64 of March 8, 1935, from the Consul General
                    at Hankow,<anchor n="62" xml:id="d786-f2"/> transmitting additional information
                    on the subject. The Department will note that the Consul General reports that
                    the Syndicate is not now, so far as known, negotiating with any firm with regard
                    to the granting of monopoly rights for the sale or export of antimony, and that
                    the Syndicate is now in a weak financial position.</p>
                <p>As of possible further interest, there is also enclosed herewith a copy of
                    despatch No. L-637 Diplomatic of March 2, 1935, from Counselor Peck<anchor
                        n="62" xml:id="d786-f3"/> concerning the same subject. It may be observed
                    that, despite the fact that Mr. Peck on March 2 urged Dr. S. S. Liu of the
                    Department of European and American Affairs of the Foreign Office to take some
                    action on the Legation’s note of February 11, no reply to that communication has
                    yet been received. Mr. Peck is being directed to inquire again of the Foreign
                    Office when a reply may be expected.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Nelson Trusler
                    Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
